  "findings": [
    "All 13 prompt tokens were normal with short input. Long input produced discrepancies for laptop, wrench, rocket, flag, ZWJ emoji, skin-tone emoji, and decomposed Hangul jamo.",
    "ASCII, composed Hangul, Chinese, accented e, combining-accent e, and the tested heart variation sequence were normal in the tested prompt cases.",
    "Emoji prompt corruption persists with plugin order reversed, either plugin alone, agnoster alone, and plain zsh. All corresponding ASCII controls were normal.",
    "At both 95 and 140 columns, the simple emoji prompt reproduced at the wrap boundary and one character beyond; one character before remained normal.",
    "With an ASCII prompt, long decomposed Hangul, laptop emoji and ZWJ command text showed discrepancies via completed-text input and app paste. Composed Hangul and combining-accent input were normal.",
    "Resizing narrower/wider, increasing font size and splitting the pane temporarily restored the emoji command display; subsequent editing reproduced corruption. Tab switching preserved corruption.",
    "Menlo and bundled Cascadia Mono settings both reproduced emoji prompt corruption. Font selection is not an established remedy.",
    "Ctrl+L restored all tested prompt-character cases, but did not restore every wrap-boundary and Unicode-input case. It is not a general fix.",
    "The real Passport SSH client to a loopback server backed by a macOS zsh PTY reproduced both plain and original-plugin emoji cases; ASCII controls were normal.",
    "Multiline paste with the emoji prompt displayed lline-one while the actual buffer began line-one; the ASCII prompt control was normal.",
    "Vim displayed head-💻 -tail for head-💻-tail. Replacing the emoji with ASCII produced the expected screen and saved head-Z-tail. No saved-file corruption in these fixtures.",
    "All 84 shell-buffer scenarios retained expected input and both Vim fixtures saved expected contents; untested conditions are not covered."
  ],
